1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,008
What's today's video about? It's about life in the Japanese countryside.

2
00:00:10,008 --> 00:00:11,508
Scene 1: Neighborly Greetings / Introduction After Moving In

3
00:00:11,508 --> 00:00:13,041
Hello there!

4
00:00:13,064 --> 00:00:16,064
Hi there!

5
00:00:16,087 --> 00:00:19,353
I came by to say hello after moving in.

6
00:00:19,376 --> 00:00:22,413
Oh, thank you so much!

7
00:00:22,437 --> 00:00:27,124
I just moved into the old house next door yesterday.

8
00:00:27,148 --> 00:00:29,215
I see.

9
00:00:29,238 --> 00:00:31,848
What's your name?

10
00:00:31,871 --> 00:00:34,004
I'm Mike.

11
00:00:34,027 --> 00:00:36,481
I'm from America.

12
00:00:36,504 --> 00:00:38,675
Mike, right?

13
00:00:38,698 --> 00:00:41,735
Your Japanese is really good!

14
00:00:41,759 --> 00:00:46,400
Oh no, I'm still not very good.

15
00:00:46,424 --> 00:00:50,499
Where in America are you from?

16
00:00:50,522 --> 00:00:52,997
I'm from New York.

17
00:00:53,021 --> 00:00:54,892
New York?

18
00:00:54,916 --> 00:00:57,649
That's a big city!

19
00:00:57,672 --> 00:01:01,919
Yes, it's a very big city.

20
00:01:01,943 --> 00:01:05,209
Why did you come to this village?

21
00:01:05,232 --> 00:01:09,365
I love the quiet countryside of Japan.

22
00:01:09,388 --> 00:01:11,388
I see.

23
00:01:11,411 --> 00:01:13,978
That's wonderful to hear!

24
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:18,168
This house is so spacious and old.

25
00:01:18,191 --> 00:01:22,067
Yes, it was built 100 years ago.

26
00:01:22,090 --> 00:01:26,690
100 years ago? That's amazing!

27
00:01:26,713 --> 00:01:29,646
Is this your first time living in the countryside?

28
00:01:29,669 --> 00:01:33,079
Yes, it's my first time.

29
00:01:33,103 --> 00:01:36,437
Was your place in New York spacious too?

30
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:41,660
No, my apartment in New York was small.

31
00:01:41,684 --> 00:01:43,450
I see.

32
00:01:43,473 --> 00:01:46,887
This place has so many rooms!

33
00:01:46,911 --> 00:01:50,778
Yes, there are 8 rooms in total.

34
00:01:50,801 --> 00:01:53,367
Do you live here alone?

35
00:01:53,390 --> 00:01:56,864
Yes, I live here by myself.

36
00:01:56,887 --> 00:02:00,254
Is your family still in America?

37
00:02:00,277 --> 00:02:04,057
Yes, my parents and sister are there.

38
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:06,888
Is there anything you're struggling with?

39
00:02:06,911 --> 00:02:10,968
I don't know where the shops are yet.

40
00:02:10,991 --> 00:02:14,525
There's a small shop nearby.

41
00:02:14,548 --> 00:02:17,434
What time does it open?

42
00:02:17,457 --> 00:02:20,224
It opens at 9 in the morning.

43
00:02:20,247 --> 00:02:23,299
Got it. I'll check it out.

44
00:02:23,322 --> 00:02:26,211
They have bread and milk there.

45
00:02:26,234 --> 00:02:28,901
That's great!

46
00:02:28,924 --> 00:02:30,390
That'll be a big help.

47
00:02:30,413 --> 00:02:33,013
Feel free to ask me anything!

48
00:02:33,036 --> 00:02:36,648
Okay, thank you so much!

49
00:02:36,672 --> 00:02:45,681
You can download today's video script from the description and top comment. Please leave a comment before downloading!

50
00:02:45,681 --> 00:02:47,181
Scene 2: A Gift of Vegetables / Giving and Receiving Vegetables

51
00:02:47,181 --> 00:02:50,115
Hello, Mike!

52
00:02:50,138 --> 00:02:52,938
Hi! How are you?

53
00:02:52,961 --> 00:02:56,327
Yes, I'm doing well.

54
00:02:56,350 --> 00:02:59,484
We harvested some vegetables from the field.

55
00:02:59,507 --> 00:03:02,576
Wow, that's a lot of vegetables!

56
00:03:02,599 --> 00:03:05,015
Here, take these.

57
00:03:05,038 --> 00:03:07,056
Please, go ahead and eat.

58
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:10,748
Are you sure? Thank you so much!

59
00:03:10,772 --> 00:03:14,138
These are tomatoes, cucumbers, and eggplants.

60
00:03:14,161 --> 00:03:16,764
They all look delicious!

61
00:03:16,788 --> 00:03:20,435
I picked them this morning from the field.

62
00:03:20,458 --> 00:03:23,309
They're so fresh!

63
00:03:23,332 --> 00:03:27,402
Did you eat vegetables in America too?

64
00:03:27,425 --> 00:03:30,858
Yes, but I bought them at the supermarket.

65
00:03:30,881 --> 00:03:33,458
Don't you grow your own?

66
00:03:33,482 --> 00:03:37,938
There was no field at my house in New York.

67
00:03:37,961 --> 00:03:39,894
I see.

68
00:03:39,917 --> 00:03:43,851
Everyone grows their own in the countryside.

69
00:03:43,874 --> 00:03:46,567
That's interesting!

70
00:03:46,590 --> 00:03:49,832
Mike, do you cook?

71
00:03:49,856 --> 00:03:53,022
Yes, I cook sometimes.

72
00:03:53,045 --> 00:03:56,289
How do you eat tomatoes?

73
00:03:56,313 --> 00:03:58,313
I make them into a salad.

74
00:03:58,336 --> 00:04:01,936
Try eating cucumbers with a bit of salt.

75
00:04:01,959 --> 00:04:05,226
With salt? I'll give it a try.

76
00:04:05,249 --> 00:04:08,416
Eggplants are delicious as tempura.

77
00:04:08,439 --> 00:04:11,572
Tempura? I love it!

78
00:04:11,595 --> 00:04:14,762
Have you ever made tempura?

79
00:04:14,785 --> 00:04:18,260
No, I've never made it.

80
00:04:18,283 --> 00:04:21,620
I'll teach you how to make it next time.

81
00:04:21,644 --> 00:04:24,610
Really? I'd appreciate that!

82
00:04:24,633 --> 00:04:28,000
Make sure to cook a lot, okay?

83
00:04:28,023 --> 00:04:30,990
Okay, I'll cook some today.

84
00:04:31,013 --> 00:04:34,179
Let me know if you can't finish it all.

85
00:04:34,202 --> 00:04:37,038
If I can't finish it all?

86
00:04:37,062 --> 00:04:40,997
Yes, I'll cook some and bring it over.

87
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:44,954
Really? That's so kind of you!

88
00:04:44,977 --> 00:04:47,746
That's just how we do things in the countryside.

89
00:04:47,769 --> 00:04:49,569
Thank you.

90
00:04:49,592 --> 00:04:51,559
I'm so happy!

91
00:04:51,582 --> 00:04:58,687
You can review today's video with the link in the description and top comment. *Note: The site may be removed without notice.

92
00:04:58,687 --> 00:05:00,187
Scene 3: How to Use an Unmanned Store

93
00:05:00,187 --> 00:05:03,926
Mike, where are you going?

94
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:07,283
I want to go to the supermarket.

95
00:05:07,306 --> 00:05:12,043
Oh, there aren't any supermarkets nearby.

96
00:05:12,066 --> 00:05:15,523
Really, there aren't any?

97
00:05:15,547 --> 00:05:20,835
The closest supermarket is a 30-minute drive away.

98
00:05:20,858 --> 00:05:24,229
A 30-minute drive?

99
00:05:24,253 --> 00:05:27,820
I don't have a car.

100
00:05:27,843 --> 00:05:30,916
That must be tough.

101
00:05:30,939 --> 00:05:34,318
Where do you usually shop?

102
00:05:34,342 --> 00:05:37,996
There's an unmanned store around here.

103
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:40,353
An unmanned store?

104
00:05:40,376 --> 00:05:42,709
What is this?

105
00:05:42,732 --> 00:05:46,616
It's a small unmanned shop.

106
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:49,376
There's no one there?

107
00:05:49,399 --> 00:05:51,933
How do you buy things?

108
00:05:51,956 --> 00:05:56,689
Pick your vegetables, then put the money in the box.

109
00:05:56,712 --> 00:05:59,945
Put the money in the box?

110
00:05:59,968 --> 00:06:02,435
Won't it get stolen?

111
00:06:02,458 --> 00:06:05,269
No one steals here.

112
00:06:05,293 --> 00:06:07,365
Don't worry.

113
00:06:07,388 --> 00:06:09,602
That's amazing!

114
00:06:09,625 --> 00:06:12,560
We don't have these in America.

115
00:06:12,584 --> 00:06:16,317
They have cheap and delicious vegetables here.

116
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:18,474
How much are they?

117
00:06:18,497 --> 00:06:21,897
Around 100 or 200 yen.

118
00:06:21,919 --> 00:06:24,558
That's really cheap!

119
00:06:24,581 --> 00:06:29,029
Also, a mobile shop comes by.

120
00:06:29,052 --> 00:06:31,386
A mobile shop?

121
00:06:31,409 --> 00:06:35,775
The truck is packed with food.

122
00:06:35,798 --> 00:06:38,965
The truck comes to sell things?

123
00:06:38,988 --> 00:06:40,794
Yes.

124
00:06:40,818 --> 00:06:44,074
It comes on Tuesdays and Fridays.

125
00:06:44,097 --> 00:06:47,591
Do they have bread and meat too?

126
00:06:47,615 --> 00:06:51,085
They have fish and tofu as well.

127
00:06:51,108 --> 00:06:54,121
Do they have snacks and drinks too?

128
00:06:54,144 --> 00:06:55,678
Yes.

129
00:06:55,701 --> 00:06:58,506
They have fruit too.

130
00:06:58,530 --> 00:07:01,663
That's so convenient!

131
00:07:01,686 --> 00:07:05,253
It's coming today at 2 PM.

132
00:07:05,276 --> 00:07:07,609
Does it make a sound?

133
00:07:07,632 --> 00:07:09,205
Yes.

134
00:07:09,229 --> 00:07:13,062
When you hear the music, come outside.

135
00:07:13,085 --> 00:07:15,118
Got it.

136
00:07:15,141 --> 00:07:17,512
I'll wait for the music.

137
00:07:17,536 --> 00:07:20,469
Let's go shopping together.

138
00:07:20,492 --> 00:07:22,189
Yes.

139
00:07:22,213 --> 00:07:24,016
Sure, let's do that.

140
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:25,540
Scene 4: Rural vs. Urban Bus Frequency

141
00:07:25,540 --> 00:07:30,073
Mike, what are you doing at the bus stop?

142
00:07:30,096 --> 00:07:33,429
I'm waiting for the bus.

143
00:07:33,452 --> 00:07:37,185
The bus just left.

144
00:07:37,208 --> 00:07:40,542
What? Really?

145
00:07:40,565 --> 00:07:45,413
Yes. The next one is in an hour.

146
00:07:45,436 --> 00:07:48,170
An hour from now?

147
00:07:48,193 --> 00:07:51,252
Only one bus per hour?

148
00:07:51,276 --> 00:07:56,123
Yes, rural buses don't come very often.

149
00:07:56,146 --> 00:08:01,631
In New York, buses come every five minutes.

150
00:08:01,654 --> 00:08:03,588
Every five minutes?

151
00:08:03,611 --> 00:08:06,377
That's fast!

152
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:09,367
I have to wait an hour?

153
00:08:09,390 --> 00:08:11,524
That's a problem.

154
00:08:11,548 --> 00:08:14,121
Where do you want to go?

155
00:08:14,144 --> 00:08:17,014
I want to go to the station.

156
00:08:17,038 --> 00:08:20,213
Are you meeting someone at the station?

157
00:08:20,236 --> 00:08:25,296
No, I'm heading to the big city by train.

158
00:08:25,319 --> 00:08:28,007
Going shopping?

159
00:08:28,030 --> 00:08:31,997
Yeah, I need to buy some clothes and books.

160
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:34,820
Did you check the timetable?

161
00:08:34,842 --> 00:08:39,409
I did, but I didn’t really understand it.

162
00:08:39,433 --> 00:08:42,799
Let me show you how to read it.

163
00:08:42,822 --> 00:08:44,598
Sure, let's do that.

164
00:08:44,622 --> 00:08:47,688
These are the weekday times.

165
00:08:47,711 --> 00:08:49,478
Yes.

166
00:08:49,501 --> 00:08:52,741
And these are the Saturday times.

167
00:08:52,765 --> 00:08:56,398
Where’s Sunday?

168
00:08:56,421 --> 00:09:00,188
There are no buses on Sundays.

169
00:09:00,211 --> 00:09:05,260
Wait, no buses on Sundays?

170
00:09:05,283 --> 00:09:09,016
Yep, they’re off that day.

171
00:09:09,039 --> 00:09:11,850
That must be tough.

172
00:09:11,873 --> 00:09:14,772
Should I use my bike?

173
00:09:14,796 --> 00:09:17,563
I have one.

174
00:09:17,586 --> 00:09:21,386
It’s a 20-minute ride to the station by bike.

175
00:09:21,409 --> 00:09:25,476
That’s faster than waiting for the bus.

176
00:09:25,499 --> 00:09:29,343
Want me to drive you today?

177
00:09:29,367 --> 00:09:31,300
Really?

178
00:09:31,323 --> 00:09:33,690
That'll be a big help.

179
00:09:33,713 --> 00:09:37,532
By car, it’s only 10 minutes.

180
00:09:37,555 --> 00:09:39,161
Thank you.

181
00:09:39,185 --> 00:09:41,520
I’d love a ride!

182
00:09:41,544 --> 00:09:43,044
Scene 5: Dealing with Bugs / How to Handle Insects

183
00:09:43,044 --> 00:09:46,329
Mike, what’s wrong?

184
00:09:46,352 --> 00:09:49,223
H-help me!

185
00:09:49,246 --> 00:09:51,328
What happened?

186
00:09:51,351 --> 00:09:55,442
There’s a huge bug in my room!

187
00:09:55,466 --> 00:09:57,948
A huge bug?

188
00:09:57,971 --> 00:10:00,138
Let me check it out.

189
00:10:00,161 --> 00:10:02,100
Over there!

190
00:10:02,124 --> 00:10:03,757
It’s on the wall.

191
00:10:03,780 --> 00:10:06,914
Oh, it’s a centipede.

192
00:10:06,937 --> 00:10:08,908
A centipede?

193
00:10:08,931 --> 00:10:11,105
Is it poisonous?

194
00:10:11,128 --> 00:10:13,613
A little, yeah.

195
00:10:13,636 --> 00:10:15,810
Don’t touch it.

196
00:10:15,834 --> 00:10:17,726
That’s terrifying!

197
00:10:17,750 --> 00:10:19,516
What should we do?

198
00:10:19,539 --> 00:10:22,198
I’ll use this spray.

199
00:10:22,221 --> 00:10:25,255
The spray that goes *pshh*?

200
00:10:25,278 --> 00:10:26,717
Yes.

201
00:10:26,741 --> 00:10:29,038
This’ll take care of it.

202
00:10:29,061 --> 00:10:30,948
Thank you.

203
00:10:30,972 --> 00:10:33,027
You’re a lifesaver!

204
00:10:33,050 --> 00:10:36,319
Don’t you have them in American homes?

205
00:10:36,343 --> 00:10:39,756
Not in my Manhattan apartment.

206
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:43,813
Old Japanese houses are made of wood and earth, after all.

207
00:10:43,836 --> 00:10:46,691
So bugs can get in easily, huh?

208
00:10:46,715 --> 00:10:48,548
Exactly.

209
00:10:48,571 --> 00:10:51,204
And the mountains are close by.

210
00:10:51,227 --> 00:10:53,761
Do other bugs show up too?

211
00:10:53,784 --> 00:10:57,680
In summer, you’ll see stink bugs and spiders.

212
00:10:57,703 --> 00:11:00,797
Stink bugs—the smelly ones?

213
00:11:00,821 --> 00:11:02,555
Yes.

214
00:11:02,578 --> 00:11:05,476
It smells really bad if you touch it.

215
00:11:05,499 --> 00:11:07,866
How do I get it outside?

216
00:11:07,889 --> 00:11:12,387
Place it on a piece of paper and gently take it outside.

217
00:11:12,410 --> 00:11:14,743
Should I not swat it?

218
00:11:14,766 --> 00:11:16,200
Yes.

219
00:11:16,223 --> 00:11:19,223
If you swat it, it’ll smell even worse.

220
00:11:19,246 --> 00:11:20,862
I see.

221
00:11:20,885 --> 00:11:22,928
I’ll remember that.

222
00:11:22,951 --> 00:11:25,569
Let’s keep the windows closed.

223
00:11:25,593 --> 00:11:27,059
Yes.

224
00:11:27,082 --> 00:11:29,649
Make sure the screens are shut tight too.

225
00:11:29,672 --> 00:11:32,739
We should buy some bug repellent.

226
00:11:32,762 --> 00:11:34,795
Where can I get some?

227
00:11:34,818 --> 00:11:37,390
They sell it at the drugstore.

228
00:11:37,414 --> 00:11:39,814
I’ll go buy some today.

229
00:11:39,837 --> 00:11:41,337
Scene 6: Village Cleanup Day

230
00:11:41,337 --> 00:11:45,937
Mike, are you busy tomorrow morning?

231
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:49,575
No, I’m free.

232
00:11:49,598 --> 00:11:51,211
Why do you ask?

233
00:11:51,235 --> 00:11:55,814
Tomorrow is the village cleanup day.

234
00:11:55,837 --> 00:11:58,070
Cleanup day?

235
00:11:58,093 --> 00:12:00,266
What is this?

236
00:12:00,289 --> 00:12:04,575
We’ll all pick up litter on the roads.

237
00:12:04,598 --> 00:12:06,732
I see.

238
00:12:06,755 --> 00:12:09,634
What time does it start?

239
00:12:09,657 --> 00:12:12,457
At 7 in the morning.

240
00:12:12,481 --> 00:12:15,373
7 AM?

241
00:12:15,397 --> 00:12:17,964
That’s early!

242
00:12:17,987 --> 00:12:22,793
Yeah, we do it before it gets too hot.

243
00:12:22,817 --> 00:12:25,217
Where do we meet?

244
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:28,773
We’ll gather in front of the park.

245
00:12:28,796 --> 00:12:31,363
What should I bring?

246
00:12:31,386 --> 00:12:35,605
Please bring work gloves and a trash bag.

247
00:12:35,628 --> 00:12:37,228
Gunte?

248
00:12:37,251 --> 00:12:40,051
What are work gloves?

249
00:12:40,074 --> 00:12:43,674
They’re white cloth gloves.

250
00:12:43,697 --> 00:12:45,914
Got it.

251
00:12:45,937 --> 00:12:48,510
Will we also cut grass?

252
00:12:48,534 --> 00:12:50,148
Yes.

253
00:12:50,171 --> 00:12:53,138
We’ll trim the grass near the river.

254
00:12:53,161 --> 00:12:56,161
Do we need tools?

255
00:12:56,184 --> 00:12:59,665
I’ll lend you a big pair of shears.

256
00:12:59,689 --> 00:13:01,555
Thank you.

257
00:13:01,578 --> 00:13:05,013
Make sure to wear rubber boots.

258
00:13:05,037 --> 00:13:07,171
Rubber boots?

259
00:13:07,194 --> 00:13:09,492
I have a pair.

260
00:13:09,515 --> 00:13:12,327
That's great!

261
00:13:12,350 --> 00:13:15,428
Will everyone from the village come?

262
00:13:15,452 --> 00:13:17,222
Yes.

263
00:13:17,245 --> 00:13:20,184
About 20 people will gather.

264
00:13:20,207 --> 00:13:24,207
We didn’t have events like this in America.

265
00:13:24,230 --> 00:13:27,697
We clean up the village together.

266
00:13:27,720 --> 00:13:31,732
That’s a wonderful tradition.

267
00:13:31,756 --> 00:13:35,164
After cleaning, we’ll have tea.

268
00:13:35,187 --> 00:13:36,874
Tea?

269
00:13:36,898 --> 00:13:39,031
That sounds nice.

270
00:13:39,054 --> 00:13:42,546
You can make friends with the villagers.

271
00:13:42,570 --> 00:13:45,255
That’s great.

272
00:13:45,278 --> 00:13:47,644
I’m looking forward to it!

273
00:13:47,667 --> 00:13:51,701
I'll come get you tomorrow morning.

274
00:13:51,724 --> 00:13:54,198
I’d appreciate that. Thank you.

275
00:13:54,221 --> 00:13:55,721
Scene 7: Tea and Pickles

276
00:13:55,721 --> 00:13:59,088
Hello, Mike!

277
00:13:59,111 --> 00:14:01,477
May I come in?

278
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:06,434
Yes, please. Come on in.

279
00:14:06,457 --> 00:14:09,354
I made some pickles.

280
00:14:09,377 --> 00:14:12,144
I brought them over.

281
00:14:12,167 --> 00:14:14,734
Pickles?

282
00:14:14,757 --> 00:14:16,523
Thank you.

283
00:14:16,546 --> 00:14:19,788
Let’s enjoy them with some tea.

284
00:14:19,812 --> 00:14:21,746
That sounds lovely.

285
00:14:21,769 --> 00:14:24,405
I’ll pour the tea.

286
00:14:24,428 --> 00:14:27,518
Would you like it hot?

287
00:14:27,541 --> 00:14:31,708
Yes, I love warm green tea.

288
00:14:31,731 --> 00:14:35,764
These are daikon pickles.

289
00:14:35,787 --> 00:14:39,636
They’re so white and pretty.

290
00:14:39,659 --> 00:14:44,096
They’re a little tangy, but delicious.

291
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:46,214
Let’s dig in.

292
00:14:46,238 --> 00:14:50,049
Mmm, this is good.

293
00:14:50,072 --> 00:14:52,691
I’m glad you like it.

294
00:14:52,714 --> 00:14:55,447
Does it suit your taste?

295
00:14:55,470 --> 00:14:59,737
Yes, it’s nice and crunchy!

296
00:14:59,760 --> 00:15:03,001
I made them myself.

297
00:15:03,025 --> 00:15:05,714
You made them yourself?

298
00:15:05,738 --> 00:15:08,071
That’s impressive!

299
00:15:08,094 --> 00:15:12,743
Every grandmother in the countryside can make them.

300
00:15:12,766 --> 00:15:17,166
In America, we eat pickles.

301
00:15:17,189 --> 00:15:19,563
Pickles?

302
00:15:19,587 --> 00:15:22,454
They’re like tsukemono, right?

303
00:15:22,477 --> 00:15:26,877
Yes, but the flavor is a little different.

304
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:31,105
Because we use Japanese soy sauce and salt.

305
00:15:31,128 --> 00:15:35,584
I see, they sound healthy.

306
00:15:35,607 --> 00:15:40,253
Oh yes, they’re very good for you.

307
00:15:40,276 --> 00:15:43,152
Do you eat them every day?

308
00:15:43,176 --> 00:15:48,709
Yes, I always have them with breakfast.

309
00:15:48,732 --> 00:15:52,369
Could you teach me how to make them?

310
00:15:52,393 --> 00:15:56,996
Of course! It’s easy.

311
00:15:57,020 --> 00:16:00,253
When can you show me?

312
00:16:00,276 --> 00:16:03,876
Let’s make them together next week.

313
00:16:03,899 --> 00:16:06,699
I'm so happy!

314
00:16:06,722 --> 00:16:09,289
I’m really looking forward to it.

315
00:16:09,312 --> 00:16:13,318
I’ll pick some daikon from the garden for us.

316
00:16:13,342 --> 00:16:17,508
From the garden? That’s amazing!

317
00:16:17,531 --> 00:16:20,398
Would you like more tea?

318
00:16:20,421 --> 00:16:24,038
Yes, please.

319
00:16:24,061 --> 00:16:25,561
Scene 8: Preparing for the Festival

320
00:16:25,561 --> 00:16:29,928
Mike, autumn is just around the corner, isn’t it?

321
00:16:29,951 --> 00:16:34,518
Yes, the breeze has gotten cooler.

322
00:16:34,541 --> 00:16:39,474
Next Sunday is the shrine festival.

323
00:16:39,497 --> 00:16:41,830
A festival?

324
00:16:41,853 --> 00:16:44,261
That sounds like fun!

325
00:16:44,285 --> 00:16:47,652
We’ll prepare for it on Saturday.

326
00:16:47,675 --> 00:16:50,408
Could you lend me a hand?

327
00:16:50,431 --> 00:16:52,570
Of course!

328
00:16:52,593 --> 00:16:55,332
What can I help you with?

329
00:16:55,356 --> 00:16:58,189
We're decorating lanterns.

330
00:16:58,212 --> 00:17:00,214
Lanterns?

331
00:17:00,238 --> 00:17:03,238
What are lanterns?

332
00:17:03,261 --> 00:17:07,028
They're lights made of paper.

333
00:17:07,051 --> 00:17:09,509
They're red and round.

334
00:17:09,533 --> 00:17:11,909
Ah, I know what you mean!

335
00:17:11,932 --> 00:17:14,066
They're beautiful.

336
00:17:14,089 --> 00:17:18,302
After that, we'll wash the mikoshi.

337
00:17:18,325 --> 00:17:20,492
The mikoshi?

338
00:17:20,515 --> 00:17:24,759
Yes, it's a vehicle for the gods.

339
00:17:24,783 --> 00:17:27,787
Is the mikoshi heavy?

340
00:17:27,810 --> 00:17:30,748
Very heavy!

341
00:17:30,772 --> 00:17:33,449
We carry it together.

342
00:17:33,473 --> 00:17:37,006
Can I help carry it on the day?

343
00:17:37,029 --> 00:17:41,729
Sure, Mike, please join us!

344
00:17:41,752 --> 00:17:44,885
Wow, I'd love to try!

345
00:17:44,908 --> 00:17:47,912
We wear special clothes.

346
00:17:47,936 --> 00:17:50,336
Happi coats.

347
00:17:50,359 --> 00:17:52,293
Happi coats?

348
00:17:52,316 --> 00:17:54,550
They look cool!

349
00:17:54,573 --> 00:17:58,573
I'll prepare a blue happi coat for you.

350
00:17:58,596 --> 00:18:00,529
Thank you.

351
00:18:00,552 --> 00:18:02,796
I’m looking forward to it!

352
00:18:02,820 --> 00:18:06,257
Do you have festivals in America?

353
00:18:06,280 --> 00:18:08,680
We have parades.

354
00:18:08,703 --> 00:18:12,108
But no mikoshi.

355
00:18:12,132 --> 00:18:16,665
Japanese festivals are so lively!

356
00:18:16,688 --> 00:18:18,728
Do they have food stalls too?

357
00:18:18,752 --> 00:18:23,368
Yes! There are takoyaki and yakisoba stalls.

358
00:18:23,391 --> 00:18:25,470
That sounds delicious!

359
00:18:25,493 --> 00:18:27,942
I want to try them.

360
00:18:27,965 --> 00:18:31,264
There are fireworks at night too!

361
00:18:31,288 --> 00:18:33,455
Fireworks?

362
00:18:33,478 --> 00:18:35,811
That sounds amazing!

363
00:18:35,834 --> 00:18:39,634
Please take lots of photos!

364
00:18:39,657 --> 00:18:42,591
I'll show them to my family in America.

365
00:18:42,614 --> 00:18:46,790
Please come to the shrine at 9 AM this Saturday.

366
00:18:46,813 --> 00:18:50,246
Yes, I'll definitely be there!

367
00:18:50,269 --> 00:18:51,769
Winter Cold and Wood Stoves

368
00:18:51,769 --> 00:18:56,369
Mike, it's gotten cold lately, hasn't it?

369
00:18:56,392 --> 00:19:01,130
Yes, mornings and nights are really chilly.

370
00:19:01,154 --> 00:19:05,425
Winters in the countryside get even colder.

371
00:19:05,448 --> 00:19:07,887
Does it snow?

372
00:19:07,911 --> 00:19:11,006
Yes, it snows a lot.

373
00:19:11,029 --> 00:19:13,862
Is it cold inside the house too?

374
00:19:13,886 --> 00:19:18,760
Yes, old houses let in drafts.

375
00:19:18,783 --> 00:19:21,116
Drafts?

376
00:19:21,139 --> 00:19:23,424
What should we do?

377
00:19:23,448 --> 00:19:26,381
Let's use a wood stove.

378
00:19:26,404 --> 00:19:28,604
A wood stove?

379
00:19:28,627 --> 00:19:32,194
You mean a stove that burns wood?

380
00:19:32,217 --> 00:19:35,785
Yes, it’s really warm.

381
00:19:35,809 --> 00:19:38,342
Where do you get the firewood?

382
00:19:38,365 --> 00:19:41,639
There’s plenty of firewood in the yard.

383
00:19:41,662 --> 00:19:45,239
So we bring this inside the house?

384
00:19:45,263 --> 00:19:48,530
Yep, every single day.

385
00:19:48,553 --> 00:19:51,166
That looks pretty heavy.

386
00:19:51,189 --> 00:19:56,163
It is, but it’s good exercise.

387
00:19:56,187 --> 00:20:00,454
In New York, my place had central heating.

388
00:20:00,477 --> 00:20:02,736
What is this?

389
00:20:02,759 --> 00:20:07,992
Just press a button, and the whole room gets warm.

390
00:20:08,015 --> 00:20:10,815
Wow, that’s convenient.

391
00:20:10,838 --> 00:20:14,073
Country life keeps you active.

392
00:20:14,097 --> 00:20:17,864
Do you split the firewood yourself too?

393
00:20:17,887 --> 00:20:20,853
Yes, I split it with an axe.

394
00:20:20,876 --> 00:20:24,893
An axe? That sounds a little tricky.

395
00:20:24,916 --> 00:20:28,483
Don’t worry, I’ll teach you the trick.

396
00:20:28,506 --> 00:20:30,977
I’ll give it a try.

397
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,680
Would you like to try a kotatsu too?

398
00:20:33,704 --> 00:20:35,937
A kotatsu?

399
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:37,994
What’s that like?

400
00:20:38,017 --> 00:20:41,095
It’s a warm table.

401
00:20:41,118 --> 00:20:44,956
Sitting under a kotatsu and eating mandarin oranges is the best.

402
00:20:44,980 --> 00:20:47,513
That sounds amazing.

403
00:20:47,536 --> 00:20:49,803
I’d love to try it.

404
00:20:49,826 --> 00:20:53,559
I’ll bring one over for you tomorrow.

405
00:20:53,582 --> 00:20:55,249
Really?

406
00:20:55,272 --> 00:20:57,672
Thank you.

407
00:20:57,695 --> 00:20:59,195
Scene 10: The Beauty of Starry Skies

408
00:20:59,195 --> 00:21:03,761
Mike, the sky is so beautiful tonight.

409
00:21:03,784 --> 00:21:06,042
It really is.

410
00:21:06,066 --> 00:21:10,032
You can see so many stars.

411
00:21:10,055 --> 00:21:13,222
The air is so clean out here in the countryside.

412
00:21:13,245 --> 00:21:17,323
You couldn’t see stars like this in New York.

413
00:21:17,347 --> 00:21:19,422
Why not?

414
00:21:19,445 --> 00:21:23,612
The city lights were too bright.

415
00:21:23,635 --> 00:21:25,402
I see.

416
00:21:25,425 --> 00:21:29,592
It gets pitch black here at night.

417
00:21:29,615 --> 00:21:33,274
At first, the darkness was a little scary.

418
00:21:33,297 --> 00:21:36,265
How about now?

419
00:21:36,289 --> 00:21:40,165
Now I love the quiet.

420
00:21:40,188 --> 00:21:43,081
Have you gotten used to country life?

421
00:21:43,105 --> 00:21:44,783
Yes.

422
00:21:44,806 --> 00:21:47,374
Every day is so enjoyable.

423
00:21:47,398 --> 00:21:50,454
The daytime scenery must be lovely too.

424
00:21:50,477 --> 00:21:51,965
Yes.

425
00:21:51,989 --> 00:21:55,356
The colors of the mountains are so beautiful.

426
00:21:55,379 --> 00:21:59,779
They’re green in spring and red in autumn.

427
00:21:59,802 --> 00:22:03,574
You can really see the change of seasons.

428
00:22:03,597 --> 00:22:07,964
The river water is so cold and clear too.

429
00:22:07,987 --> 00:22:10,387
Are there fish in it?

430
00:22:10,410 --> 00:22:11,863
Yes.

431
00:22:11,886 --> 00:22:15,854
Little fish swim around all the time.

432
00:22:15,878 --> 00:22:19,044
I’d love to try fishing in the river sometime.

433
00:22:19,067 --> 00:22:20,834
That sounds lovely.

434
00:22:20,857 --> 00:22:23,835
I’ll lend you some fishing gear.

435
00:22:23,858 --> 00:22:25,459
Thank you.

436
00:22:25,483 --> 00:22:29,661
You’ve really gotten close with the neighbors too, haven’t you?

437
00:22:29,685 --> 00:22:33,051
Yes, everyone here is so kind.

438
00:22:33,074 --> 00:22:37,120
It's because Mike always greets us with a smile.

439
00:22:37,144 --> 00:22:39,877
Greetings are so important, aren't they?

440
00:22:39,900 --> 00:22:43,467
Do you ever think about moving back to the city?

441
00:22:43,490 --> 00:22:47,580
No, not at all.

442
00:22:47,603 --> 00:22:50,570
I'm relieved to hear that.

443
00:22:50,593 --> 00:22:53,926
I want to live here forever.

444
00:22:53,949 --> 00:22:58,483
Let's head to the lookout point on the mountain tomorrow.

445
00:22:58,506 --> 00:23:00,472
A lookout point?

446
00:23:00,495 --> 00:23:03,262
You mean a place where you can see the view?

447
00:23:03,285 --> 00:23:05,018
Yes.

448
00:23:05,041 --> 00:23:07,849
You can see the whole village from there.

449
00:23:07,873 --> 00:23:10,007
I'd love to go!

450
00:23:10,030 --> 00:23:12,301
How are we getting there?

451
00:23:12,324 --> 00:23:14,991
Let's go by bicycle.

452
00:23:15,014 --> 00:23:16,780
That sounds lovely.

453
00:23:16,803 --> 00:23:19,416
I’m really looking forward to it.